From: =?UTF-8?q?=EC=8B=AC=EC=83=81=EC=9B=90?= <dev.chamomile@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 23:24:22 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] jailer: Linux-only primitives behind a backend per host

Why: upstream's jailer uses Linux-only primitives (pivot_root in a mount
namespace, cgroup files, setns, clone(CLONE_NEWPID), device nodes, the
exec as uid:gid, close_range), so it did not build on macOS and a macOS
Firecracker ran with no jail: no chroot, no uid/gid drop, no memory
limit.

What: upstream's jailer (command line, Env::new, Env::run) runs
unchanged on every host. Outside Linux its chroot and cgroup modules,
and the Env steps that need Linux, are backends in overlay/jailer/
platform; on Linux they stay upstream's. The macOS backend chroots with
chroot(2), turns the memory cgroup into a jetsam limit, refuses what
macOS cannot enforce (network and PID namespaces, other cgroup
controllers), needs no device nodes, and, since no program can start
inside a chroot on macOS (dyld), leaves the chroot for the exec only and
hands the jail over: Firecracker calls vmm::ext::jail::enter first,
which chroots into the same jail and drops to uid:gid. Upstream's tests
of the Linux backend stay Linux-only; the macOS backend has its own.

Test: `cargo test -p jailer` as root (as upstream's), and
tools/macos/jailer-test.sh (refusals, uid/gid, chroot, files outside
the jail, daemonize, jetsam limit and priority, a guest booted, snapshot
and restored in jails, a guest past the memory limit killed).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
